description.narrative:
The Think Big Start Small: Youth-led Robotics for Multi-dimensional Climate Solutions’, or in short ‘Youth Climate Robotics’, is a pilot project implemented over 6 months in 2021 by the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) in partnership with FEMSTEM Pty (Ltd) (hereby FEMSTEM) and Sci-Bono Discovery Centre (hereby Sci-Bono) through the support from the Embassy of Denmark in South Africa and the United Nations Children’s Fund (UNICEF) in South Africa.
This unique project was designed to target both the need for skills development in the digital economy as well as the need for increased environmental awareness, and the application of digital skills to contribute to solving energy (SDG 7), climate (SDG 13) and environmental (SDG 15)-related challenges. As such, the project objective has been to equip high school students and educators with practical skills and ideas to develop STEM solutions to chart sustainable climate futures. The project objective was reached by:
- Meaningful engagement of teachers and high school students on the meaning and intentions of the climate-oriented SDGs;
- Development and honing of technical and practical skills in robotics and chip technology;
- Facilitation of learning and skill development aligned with the future of work, and;
- Acknowledgment and championing of school-age youth, particularly girls from disadvantaged communities, in prize challenges, award ceremonies, as well as feature the developed solutions.

The overall goal of the Think Big Start Small project proposal is to equip school students and their teachers with practical skills and ideas to develop STEMI-related solutions for sustainable climate futures. The secondary goals are to:
- Meaningfully engage teachers and school students on the meaning and intentions of the climate-oriented SDGs; 
- Develop and hone technical and practical skills in climate problem-solving using robotics and chip technology; 
- Facilitate digital learning and skill development aligned with the future of work; and 
- Deliver prize challenges, award ceremonies, as well as feature the developed climate solutions.

The work will be implemented by UNDP in South Africa, with its partners, namely a company called XinaBox (Danish innovated), through its South African company, FEMSTEM (Pty) Ltd. UNDP will also collaborate with Sci-Bono Discovery Centre, a Johannesburg-based science centre and NGO; and with UNICEF.
